::GEO_U06_L05_GP01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
A rectangular prism is 6 cm by 4 cm by 5 cm. What is its volume?
{
=A. 120 cm³#Volume is lwh, so 6(4)(5)=120 cubic centimeters.
~B. 30 cm²#This adds dimensions and uses area units.
~C. 48 cm²#This multiplies only two dimensions by 2, not all three dimensions.
~D. 15 cm#This adds dimensions and uses linear units.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
A cylinder has radius 3 and height 7. Which expression gives its volume?
{
~A. 2pi(3)(7)#This resembles lateral surface area, not volume.
=B. pi(3)?(7)#Cylinder volume is pir?h.
~C. 1/3pi(3)?(7)#The one-third factor is for a cone, not a cylinder.
~D. 4pi(3)?#This is related to sphere volume, not cylinder volume.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
A cone and cylinder have the same radius and height. How does the cone volume compare to the cylinder volume?
{
~A. It is equal to the cylinder volume#A cone is not equal in volume to the matching cylinder.
~B. It is twice the cylinder volume#A cone is smaller than the matching cylinder.
=C. It is one-third of the cylinder volume#Cone volume is 1/3pir?h.
~D. It is three times the cylinder volume#This reverses the relationship.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
Which unit is appropriate for volume?
{
~A. cm#Centimeters are linear units.
~B. cm²#Square centimeters measure area or surface area.
~C. degrees#Degrees measure angles.
=D. cm³#Volume measures three-dimensional space, so cubic units are appropriate.
}

::GEO_U06_L05_GP05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L05_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.14
Question:
A sphere has radius 2. Which expression gives its volume?
{
=A. 4/3pi(2)?#Sphere volume is 4/3pir?.
~B. 4pi(2)?#This is sphere surface area, not volume.
~C. pi(2)?h#This is cylinder volume and includes height.
~D. 1/3pi(2)?h#This is cone volume and includes height.
}

